<h3>Introductory Article:</h3>


<h4>• Value</h4>


<p>


    Giac Vien Pagoda is one of the oldest and most representative Buddhist architectural temples in Southern Vietnam.<br/>


The pagoda bears deep cultural, religious, and historical significance, with a history spanning nearly 300 years.<br/>


It is a place where many unique cultural values converge, especially wood carving art, gilded parallel sentences, and ancient Buddha statues.<br/>


The pagoda serves not only as a pilgrimage site for Buddhists but also as a destination for cultural researchers and tourists.


  </p>


<div class="post-frame">


<img alt="Post image" src="/images/cgv1.jpg"/>


<div class="img-ref">Source: thanhnien.vn</div>


</div>


<h4>• Origins:</h4>


<p>


    Giac Vien Pagoda originated during the renovation of Giac Lam Pagoda in the year Mậu Ngọ (1798), when Zen Master To Tong – Vien Quang (36th generation, abbot from 1774–1827) ordered a complete reconstruction of Giac Lam.<br/>


The timber used for the renovation was transported from the forest along the Ho Dat Canal, then via the Ong Buong Canal, and was unloaded at a riverside spot — the site where Giac Vien Pagoda would later be built.<br/>


During the renovation period (1798–1804), a temple caretaker (responsible for incense offerings) built a small hermitage dedicated to Bodhisattva Avalokitesvara (Guanyin) at that location, both as a place for practice and to guard the construction materials.<br/>


When the renovation of Giac Lam Pagoda was completed, Master Vien Quang had the hermitage rebuilt into a pagoda, naming it Quan Am Institute.<br/>


In Canh Tuat year (1850), Most Venerable Tien Giac – Hai Tinh (37th generation, abbot of Giac Lam) restored the pagoda and renamed it Giac Vien Pagoda.<br/>


Two years later, after the caretaker passed away, the Most Venerable appointed Zen Master Minh Vi – Mat Hanh (38th generation) as abbot, and he established a school for Buddhist ceremonial studies at the pagoda.<br/>


In 1869, Zen Master Minh Vi was appointed abbot of Giac Lam Pagoda, and Zen Master Minh Khiem – Hoang An succeeded him as abbot of Giac Vien.<br/>


Subsequent abbots included Nhu Nhu – Chan Khong, Nhu Phong – Hoang Nghia, Hong Tu – Hue Nhon, and Thich Thien Phu, among others.<br/>


During the resistance wars against the French and Americans, the pagoda served as a shelter for revolutionary cadres and played an important role in preserving and promoting national culture.<br/>


In 1993, Giac Vien Pagoda was officially recognized as a National Architectural and Artistic Monument, honoring its distinctive historical and cultural value.


  </p>


<div class="post-frame">


<img alt="Post image" src="/images/cgv2.jpg"/>


<div class="img-ref">Source: mia.vn</div>


</div>


<h4>• Structural Values:</h4>


<p>


    Giac Vien Pagoda is a traditional wooden architectural structure built in the “nội công ngoại quốc” style — a typical layout of ancient Southern Vietnamese temples, consisting of three main halls: the front hall (tiền đường), the main sanctuary (chánh điện), and the ancestral hall (hậu tổ), all connected to form the Chinese character “Tam” (三).<br/>


The pagoda houses hundreds of Buddha statues made of wood, clay, and bronze, dating from various periods. Among them are rare and precious sculptures, such as the Reclining Shakyamuni Buddha entering Nirvana and the Thousand-Armed, Thousand-Eyed Avalokitesvara (Quan Âm Thiên Thủ Thiên Nhãn).<br/>


Its carved panels, parallel sentences, and horizontal boards are exquisitely crafted, showcasing the distinct sculptural artistry of the 18th–19th centuries.<br/>


The pagoda also preserves many valuable relics, including bronze drums, ancient bells, and Han–Nom scriptures.<br/>


With its spacious grounds and ancient trees, Giac Vien Pagoda offers a peaceful and contemplative atmosphere, ideal for pilgrimage and meditation.


  </p>


<div class="post-frame">


<img alt="Post image" src="/images/cgv3.jpg"/>


<div class="img-ref">Source: chonthieng.vn</div>


</div>


</div>


